acy by WikiLeaks\". *The New York Times*. ISSN 0362-4331. Retrieved 30 September 2023.\n346. ↑ \"What is WikiLeaks?\". Archived from the original on 1 October 2022. Retrieved 1 October 2022.\n347. ↑ Helin, Jan (14 August 2010). \"Därför blir Julian Assange kolumnist i Aftonbladet\". *Aftonbladet (blog)* (in Swedish). Stockholm. Archived from the original on 20 August 2012. Retrieved 15 August 2010.\n348. ↑ \"A Swedish Shield, Unraised\". *Columbia Journalism Review*. Retrieved 29 May 2023.\n349. ↑ Greenberg, Andy (27 September 2012). \"How Two Bulgarian Journalists Created a WikiLeaks Copycat That Actually Worked\". *Slate*. Retrieved 22 July 2022.\n350. ↑ Crawford, Jeanne Whalen And David (4 November 2010). \"WikiLeaks Faces New Competition\". *The Wall Street Journal*. Retrieved 7 February 2023.\n351. ↑ Constantin, Lucian (29 November 2011). \"WikiLeaks: Security worries impede new submission system\". *Computerworld*. Archived from the original on 22 July 2022. Retrieved 22 July 2022.\n352. 1 2 3 4 Greenberg, Andy. \"WikiLeaks Unveils 160 Firms' Surveillance Gear Sales Docs, Still No Submissions System\". *Forbes*. Archived from the original on 15 May 2023. Retrieved 27 January 2024.\n353. ↑ Kocieniewski, David (30 January 2019). \"The Civil Rights Warrior Who May Have Linked Roger Stone to WikiLeaks\". *Bloomberg*. Archived from the original on 21 September 2019. Retrieved 8 November 2019.\n354. ↑ \"Gillard condemns WikiLeaks\". The Sydney Morning Herald. AAP. 2 December 2010. Retrieved 26 March 2020.\n355. ↑ Gillard, Julia (7 December 2010). \"Gillard refines verdict on Assange\". *The World Today* (Interview). Interviewed by Lyndal Curtis. ABC Radio (Australia). Archived from the original on 9 February 2014. Retrieved 12 December 2010.\n356. ↑ Karvelas, Patricia (14 December 2010). \"Party revolt growing over Prime Minister Julia Gillard's WikiLeaks stance\". *The Australian*. Sydney. Archived from the original on 31 July 2013. Retrieved 14 December 2010.\n357